  The interface the README states, in full: (1) GOV_VECTOR_CACHE_DIR: the folder holding the machine-wide cache, created if absent. Its default is in the user's own folder (e.g. under $XDG_CACHE_HOME, else ~/.cache; the cases only check that it is under HOME or XDG_CACHE_HOME). Once every process using it has ended, the folder holds one file, the cache, mode 0600, shared by every tree and clone. No off switch is needed: "without the cache" means a build with an empty cache folder of its own. (2) GOV_VECTOR_CACHE_MAX_BYTES: a whole number of bytes, the bound on the cache, measured as the size on disk of what the cache folder holds once a write has ended. Default about two corpora (not tested). Least recently used entries (by lookup or store) go first. (3) The dicts returned by semantic.refresh(root) and semantic.search(root, question, ...) carry a key `vector_cache` holding {"hits": number of texts served from the cache in that call, "misses": number of texts sent to the embedder in that call (and stored), "skipped": None when the cache was used, else a non-empty string saying why it was skipped (unreadable, locked)}; when skipped, the texts are embedded and the call succeeds. query_vector keeps its (vector, reason) return; the cases only count what it sends. (4) The key is the model, the revision /api/tags reports (its digest) and the sha256 of the exact text sent; the cases see it only through what is sent.
discoveries: (1) /tmp/gov-os-lead-W2-126 is read-only in this session's sandbox, and so are /tmp and /var/tmp, so `TMPDIR=/tmp/gov-os-lead-W2-126` makes Python's tempfile fall back to the current folder: my first try created pytest-of-usain/ inside the worktree. (2) The base writes ~/.cache/gov-os/secrets-scan.sqlite under HOME during a lexical build; the default-location case therefore counts only the files that appear once the setting is left out. (3) ~/.local/bin holds both gitleaks and ollama, so a PATH that drops folders containing ollama loses gitleaks; the suite links gitleaks into a folder of its own. (4) The base caches no question vector for a tree that has no .gov-runtime/ folder. (5) This worktree's src (2a3c998fe786) still has the pre-DEC-1033 device rule; w2/integrate has `_device`. The cases rely only on the public surface the two share, and the stand-in reports the model wholly on the CPU, which both rules accept. (6) On this machine's real dev tiers, one retrieval-check run sends 1337 texts to the embedder.
risks: (1) The bound is checked strictly against the size on disk (≤ GOV_VECTOR_CACHE_MAX_BYTES, sidecar files included). An implementation that counts only vector bytes will fail it. (2) The "locked" case holds both an flock and a whole-file POSIX record lock. A cache that waits a long time on every lock attempt can approach the driver's 300 s limit. (3) "One file" is checked after every process has ended, so a persistent WAL file or a separate lock file fails KPI 1. (4) The emptied probe tests/acceptance/W2-126/test_zz_probe_home.py (uncommitted, no tests in it) and the worktree's untracked pytest-of-usain/ remain because `rm` was refused; the lead should delete them, together with .gov-runtime/scratch/{reg.md,p1.md} and the session TMPDIR's pytest folders.
lessons: Give test-designer sessions a writable TMPDIR. Wherever a case builds a PATH without ollama, link gitleaks separately. Expect the base's own files under HOME/.cache/gov-os.
